## Supported statement and physical observable

Fix the original integer-spin cube with lambda=0, positive fixed
delta, K, kappa and epsilon squared times S(S+1) equal to delta/K. Start at an
actual normalized first-birth state with one of the original plus, minus or
coherent marks. Subsequent evolution is the full original ensemble. On N=6,

    H_eff = H - i kappa Gamma_S/(2 epsilon^2),
    v(t) = exp(-it H_eff) phi_i.

Since the terminal N=8 Hamiltonian vanishes, the unconditioned full-ensemble
second moment is `||H v||^2`, and its mean is `<v,Hv>`. No division by survival
probability is appropriate. Write psi_i for the limiting effective low ket,
and `s_i(t)=||psi_i(t)||^2`. The frozen argument supports, for fixed
`0<a<b<infinity`,

    liminf epsilon^2 integral_a^b Var_H(rho_i(t)) dt
        >= (kappa/2) [s_i(a)-s_i(b)].

The essential low-component identity is correctly an identity for the physical
Hermitian H. With `v_low=J_0 a_0`, exact intertwining gives

    H J_0 a_0 = i J_0 K_0 a_0
                   + i kappa Gamma_S J_0 a_0/(2 epsilon^2).

The parent's weighted trajectory bound makes `||K_0 a_0||` bounded on a fixed
time interval, despite the global operator norm `||K_0||=O(epsilon^-2)`.
Together with `J_0=P+epsilon F_S P+O(epsilon^2)` and `Gamma_S P=0`, this proves

    epsilon H J_0 a_0 -> i(kappa/2) Gamma_infinity F_infinity psi_i

uniformly on compact time intervals. The stronger parity remainder in the
candidate is compatible with the parent expansions but is not needed: an
`O(epsilon^2)` remainder in `Gamma_S J_0` suffices. Uniform boundedness and
strong convergence of the normalized spin operators, applied to the compact
limiting trajectory, justify the uniform vector limit. Replacing H by H_eff
would remove this leading contribution and compute the wrong second moment.

## Rectangular Sylvester estimate

The candidate's proof of integrated cross-term cancellation is valid and uses
less high-component information than the PRE proof. For each pair j<k, work
on the Banach space of operators from the kth bare grade to the jth bare
grade, equipped with the usual Hilbert-space operator norm. Its Sylvester map
is

    L_jk(Y) = K_j* Y + Y K_k
            = i delta (j-k) epsilon^-4 Y + R_j* Y + Y R_k.

The remainder map has norm at most `||R_j||+||R_k||=O(epsilon^-2)`.
Thus, for sufficiently small epsilon,

    ||L_jk^-1|| <=
      1 / [delta |j-k| epsilon^-4 - ||R_j|| - ||R_k||]
      = O(epsilon^4).

This is a norm-convergent Neumann inverse on the rectangular operator space.
There is no conversion from Frobenius norm and no dimension-dependent factor.
It needs neither diagonalizability nor a gap inside either cluster. The only
gap used is the fixed difference of the bare grades, with delta positive and
fixed. The claimed uniform cluster remainder is an explicit inherited
hypothesis, rather than a conclusion supplied by the finite controls.

The globally valid bounds are `||H J_0||=O(epsilon^-2)` and
`||H J_1||+||H J_2||=O(epsilon^-4)`. Consequently, solving
`L_jk(Y_jk)=(H J_j)*H J_k` yields
`Y_01,Y_02=O(epsilon^-2)` and `Y_12=O(epsilon^-4)`. Exact contraction of the
physical no-event evolution, commutation with its Riesz projections and the
uniformly bounded coordinate maps propagate the initial bounds
`a_0=O(1), a_1=O(epsilon), a_2=O(epsilon^2)` to every nonnegative time.
No contraction of the coordinate evolution in its own norm is being assumed.

Differentiation gives precisely

    integral_a^b <H J_j a_j, H J_k a_k> dt
        = [<a_j,Y_jk a_k>]_a^b.

The three integral bounds are respectively `O(epsilon^-1)`, `O(1)` and
`O(epsilon^-1)`. Their products with epsilon squared vanish. Both high
diagonal squared norms remain nonnegative; discarding them is justified only
for the lower inequality. The proof neither substitutes a rotor propagator at
growing fast time nor removes a high diagonal term from an asserted equality.

## Probability coefficient and original words

In the rotor N=6 sector the complete original loss is
`Gamma_infinity=2 P_bright`, so `Gamma_infinity^2=2 Gamma_infinity`.
The factor two counts the two original resolved orientations on the adjacent
vacancy edge. The coherent description has the same complete loss: the two
orientation ranges for that edge are orthogonal. Hence, with
`Lambda=P F_infinity* Gamma_infinity F_infinity P`,

    ||Gamma_infinity F_infinity psi||^2 = 2 <psi,Lambda psi>,
    s_i'(t) = -kappa <psi_i,Lambda psi_i>.

The low second-moment coefficient is therefore exactly
`(kappa/2)[s_i(a)-s_i(b)]`. It is the effective second-birth probability
increment multiplied by kappa/2, not a probability conditioned again at time
a. The original finite-spin loss need not be a scalar projector; the
projector identity here is used only after the controlled rotor limit.

The author's exact integer words give birth norms squared 2, 2, 4 and,
after normalization, loss expectation 8 and squared loss-action norm 16 for
each initial mark. A separate POST implementation using explicit charge and
field words, checking Gauss's law after every hop and birth, reproduces all
these values. It explicitly sums all 24 resolved second marks and all 12
coherent second marks; both sums equal 8. Thus `s_i'(0)=-8 kappa` and the
initial scaled low second-moment coefficient is `4 kappa^2`.

The author's conclusion of positivity on sufficiently early fixed intervals
follows from continuity. Its conditional lower bound is valid on every fixed
positive interval. The stronger all-interval positivity assertion in the
PRE uses its additional local-star argument and is not present in the frozen
root proof.

The ordinary-energy parent supplies the bounded mean on `[a,b]` with a>0,
so `epsilon^2 integral m(t)^2 dt ->0`. This step correctly converts the
second-moment lower bound into the full variance lower bound. This argument
does not establish the corresponding subtraction through t=0.

